78128 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278128 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278128 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278128 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278128 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e75d780b-e75e-5b25-86f0-f1f9572d8822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186276851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278137 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e948b81b-2e80-5712-b122-75bcc52059e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "128c01a3-ffea-59eb-9892-8068500c0400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278390 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b62215b7-0e69-5d45-8968-8bb0a1bc12ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278392 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x+{y}^{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=1.$ Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c11e996e-10b6-5010-bc4a-0d91257a3dfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278397 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/87/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the portion of the cylinder $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbda2ff7-05a0-5600-96fa-c9dfe7d1924f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278402 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2y-x$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ and $x\\le 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd23390c-f0cc-59ec-891d-3ea7fdf2c7b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278404 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2y-x,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,x\\le 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c6c556ec90138fa1f0b599c6afed9d093e5c1b10f7751a5fbe55f0add617e1d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66ec1dd0-f28a-513c-a9f4-29ac282d0de0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278406 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7cb4d16f39d1c1e38d65000471cbc5ca1831a0bbc2cfc61c4f03d087d6a5125.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=4-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r & =-\\pi {\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2{({u}^{1/2})}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({4}^{1/2}-{3}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $x={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9$ and $z\\le 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5e36073-07ed-5be9-b9f9-14a5dbeadf43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278407 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9,\\,z\\le 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd5bcd7857769afb5f3a4f6197757c08eb295c7075a271c302c4b6087d661d14.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x=2-y-z$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d00a6aa-63ec-5be5-aa3c-4e85a0b4a1af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278408 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=2-y-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/37/3741c5db05232f04f60f747dc04176bfc77da30bbc39c79888cf4c18939369f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-y-z)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2-y-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fedbba32-290f-52d2-807e-9187e81e100a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278409 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab855ef2b9b5363005c14ea37470ed9ecb5853380a301341d20e2cd6095a5d1f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\sqrt{x},$ $x=4,$ and the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3dce2ffe-4326-5f45-948b-458dac5baa92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278410 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a4ed826b683e7e8d8ed3e05bcb75618e74314febcdda8ae31b09f379ca21c73.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)={x}^{3/4}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{3/4})}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{x}^{3/2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 4 \\\\ u & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={e}^{y}-z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z={e}^{2y},$ $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c338dfd1-e7f8-59f7-ac67-821e4b45c353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278429 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{2y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9c/9cae5b68f77c2d79df8a81d0421146ac2bbe618c6065a457c33c86caceeb1327.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}-z)={e}^{y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{y})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{2y}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z={e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u={e}^{2y}+2,$ we get $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ u & 3 & e+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{e+2}\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((e+2)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{1}{xz}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e50892b-6dbf-5e7c-ae2e-f87a4a349f42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278511 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{xz},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}{z}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186386435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386432 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc1582c5-7435-5ca4-898e-aba8007cc959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386433 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c6a3e1b6-3e3a-58d9-b55e-673dc95b3342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386434 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386434 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386434 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74e07110-fee2-57d3-a642-c06c3a73ad70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386435 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2